<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_data_collection_training_regression_evaluation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Training Loops &amp; Regression Evaluation</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Because steering a robot involves selecting a value along a continuous spectrum (e.g., steering anywhere from <m>-30^\circ</m> to <m>+30^\circ</m>), behavioral cloning operates as a <term>regression</term> task.</p>
    <p>To determine how accurately our model has cloned the expert's behavior during a training loop, we must evaluate its performance mathematically. The standard metric used to calculate this error is the <term>Mean Squared Error (MSE)</term>. MSE measures the average squared difference between the model's predicted steering values and the true expert labels across the dataset:</p>
    <me>\operatorname{MSE} = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left(y_i - \hat{y}_i\right)^2</me>
    <p>Where:</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><m>y_i</m> is the actual target command provided by the expert driver.</li>
        <li><m>\hat{y}_i</m> is the command predicted by the autonomous driving network.</li>
        <li><m>n</m> is the total number of data samples evaluated.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p><term>The Data Collection Loop</term></p>
    <p>A behavioral cloning model is only as good as the data used to train it. The end-to-end training loop follows four main steps: Record Human Drive &#8594; Preprocess Data &#8594; Train Model (Regression) &#8594; Evaluate &amp; Deploy.</p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li><p><em class="emphasis">Recording:</em> Capture synchronized camera frames (30 Hz) and control actions <m>(v, \omega)</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><em class="emphasis">Preprocessing:</em> Normalize image pixel values and scale control inputs (e.g., mapping motor values to <m>[-1.0, 1.0]</m>).</p></li>
        <li><p><em class="emphasis">Training (Regression Task):</em> Because motor outputs like velocity <m>v</m> and turn rate <m>\omega</m> are continuous numerical values, behavioral cloning treats control estimation as a regression problem. The training algorithm minimizes the difference between human actions <m>y_{\text{human}}</m> and model predictions <m>\hat{y}_{\text{model}}</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><em class="emphasis">Evaluation:</em> Calculate error metrics such as Mean Squared Error (MSE) before deploying the model to physical hardware.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_data_collection_exercises">
    <title>Section 7.2 Interactive Exercises</title>

    <figure align="center" xml:id="fig_traditional_vs_end_to_end_pipeline">
      <caption>Traditional modular pipelines chain together separate object detection and path planning stages, while end-to-end pipelines map vision input directly to motor control through a single ML model.</caption>
      <image source="ch07_end_to_end_autonomous_driving/traditional_vs_end_to_end_pipeline.png" width="100%" alt="Comparison of a traditional modular robotics pipeline with separate object detection and path planning stages versus an end-to-end pipeline that maps vision sensor input directly to motor control through a single ML model."/>
    </figure>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec_regression_evaluation_loop_activecode">
      <title>Exercise 7.2.1: ActiveCode Exercise &#8212; Building a Motor Command Regression Evaluation Loop</title>
      <p>Note: Because Runestone CodeLens runs standard Python without external machine learning libraries, we implement a pure-Python regression evaluation loop below!</p>
      <p>In this exercise, you will complete a function that evaluates a trained end-to-end control model against a mock dataset of human expert steering actions <m>(v, \omega)</m>.</p>
      <p><term>Task:</term></p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) between the human's linear velocity commands and the model's predicted commands.</p></li>
          <li><p>Print whether the model passes safety evaluation (Average Error <m>&lt; 0.1</m> m/s).</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      <program xml:id="ac_driving_model_evaluation" interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <input>
def evaluate_driving_model(human_dataset, model_predictions):
    """
    Evaluates model accuracy against expert human driver commands.
    Both inputs are lists of tuples: (linear_velocity_v, angular_velocity_w)
    """
    total_v_error = 0.0
    total_w_error = 0.0
    num_samples = len(human_dataset)

    for i in range(num_samples):
        v_human, w_human = human_dataset[i]
        v_model, w_model = model_predictions[i]

        # Calculate absolute errors
        total_v_error += abs(v_human - v_model)
        total_w_error += abs(w_human - w_model)

    mae_v = total_v_error / num_samples
    mae_w = total_w_error / num_samples

    return mae_v, mae_w

# Mock Dataset: (linear_velocity v in m/s, angular_velocity w in rad/s)
human_expert_data = [
    (0.5, 0.0),   # Drive straight
    (0.5, 0.2),   # Slight right turn
    (0.0, 0.0),   # Stop at obstacle
    (0.4, -0.3),  # Left turn
    (0.0, 0.0)    # Stop at goal
]

# Model Predictions from an End-to-End Network
model_predictions = [
    (0.48, 0.01),  # Close to straight
    (0.52, 0.18),  # Close to slight right
    (0.02, 0.00),  # Almost stopped
    (0.38, -0.28), # Close to left turn
    (0.01, 0.01)   # Almost stopped
]

# Evaluate performance
error_v, error_w = evaluate_driving_model(human_expert_data, model_predictions)

print(f"Linear Velocity MAE (v): {error_v:.4f} m/s")
print(f"Angular Velocity MAE (w): {error_w:.4f} rad/s")

# Safety Threshold Check
if error_v &lt; 0.1 and error_w &lt; 0.1:
    print("STATUS: Model Evaluation PASSED! Safe for robot deployment.")
else:
    print("STATUS: Model Evaluation FAILED! Error too high.")
</input>
      </program>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec_end_to_end_pipeline_parsons">
      <title>Exercise 7.2.2: Parsons Problem &#8212; End-to-End Data Pipeline Execution</title>
      <p>Reorder the steps below to form the correct sequence for training and deploying an end-to-end behavioral cloning robot.</p>
      <exercise xml:id="ex_pp_end_to_end_pipeline" indent="show" language="python">
        <statement>
          <p>Arrange the steps in the order they must occur to collect data, train, and deploy an end-to-end behavioral cloning model.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
          <block order="1">
            <cline>Mount camera and connect human joystick controller to robot platform.</cline>
          </block>
          <block order="2">
            <cline>Teleoperate the robot along target paths while recording (Camera Image, Motor Speed) data pairs.</cline>
          </block>
          <block order="3">
            <cline>Preprocess dataset and normalize motor output targets.</cline>
          </block>
          <block order="4">
            <cline>Train regression model to minimize difference between predicted and human motor outputs.</cline>
          </block>
          <block order="5">
            <cline>Deploy trained model to robot onboard computer to drive autonomously from camera feed.</cline>
          </block>
        </blocks>
      </exercise>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>
